{
 "research_concepts": [
  "player absence",
  "offensive role reallocation",
  "play type initiation",
  "teammate usage change",
  "player similarity",
  "player initiation profile"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "defining player initiation profile",
  "measuring play type gains",
  "identifying absence spells",
  "controlling for confounders in role changes",
  "quantifying player similarity",
  "establishing causality of role changes to absence"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-088",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Connector absence displaces team offensive identity (D_A) more than usage-matched non-organizer absence. Unmatched conn vs role D_A 0.2020 vs 0.1021, t=5.97 p=2.87e-08. Usage-matched vs role: paired dD_A +0.1151, P(conn>ctrl)=81.2%, t=6.35 p=7.35e-09 n=96. Vs terminal: +0.0777 t=2.37 p=0.0261 n=25."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
   "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
   "finding": "Similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; almost always findable. mean sim ABSORBED +0.863 vs CASCADE +0.848; Spearman(sim, -gap)=-0.19 (wrong-signed). Embiid 23-24 cascades with Reed sim 0.94."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-036",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "The team-level BEST vs AVOID gap holds when H2 possessions are filtered to initiating player. Mean gap (n>=10 each tier) +0.427 PPP. Mean BEST 1.347 (38 players) vs AVOID 0.920 (94 players). Stars mean gap +0.542 (n=15) vs role +0.428 (n=16). Only 3 players clear n>=30 both tiers (all BOS: White +0.864, Brown +0. [cut; read_family for the full text]"
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-008",
   "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
   "finding": "d_entropy_nonfocal=-0.0455 (shaper, n_ts=80); organizer +0.0115. Paper interprets as 'rebalancing not expanding'; no CI given."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF20",
   "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
   "finding": "[APPA-006] Gate G2 21/23 (91%) above null median; V1(a) 16/23 above own-null p95, binomial p=2.67e-16; V1(b) mean(D_real - median_null)=+0.142, CI [+0.103, +0.187]. [APPA-007] Gate G3 Spearman +0.099. V2a Spearman +0.288, p=0.077, CI [-0.244, +0.667] FAIL; V2b partial +0.253, p=0.115, CI [-0.262, +0.615] FAIL. [PAP-023] V1(a) 16/23 above p95 binomial p=2.67e-16; V1(b) +0.142 CI[+0.103,+0.187] CONFIRMED; V2a rho=+0.288 p=0.077 CI[-0.244,+0.667]; V2b rho=+0.253 p=0.115 NOT"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
   "what_failed": "Using similar-profile teammate to separate absorption from cascade when a player sits.",
   "why": "B_powered_null (adequately powered null result); Similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; almost always findable."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF20",
   "what_failed": "Predicting the magnitude of structural changes due to player absence using LOO simulation.",
   "why": "LIM:small_sample; V2a and V2b failed to show significant predictive power for magnitude."
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "Player absences cause measurable shifts in team offensive identity and structure (FAM:SF-APPS-088, FAM:SF20).",
  "Player-level initiation patterns can be quantified and show measurable impact (FAM:SF-APPS-036).",
  "Previous attempts to use player similarity (profile) to predict the type of role reallocation (absorption vs. cascade) after an absence were inconclusive, suggesting that similarity alone might not be a strong predictor for how roles are reallocated (FAM:SF-CHT-052).",
  "Offensive changes due to absence might involve rebalancing existing play types rather than expanding the repertoire (FAM:SF-PAP-008)."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "Whether 'closest pre-absence initiation profile' (or other proxy measures like minutes/initiations) directly predicts which teammate gains the largest share of the absent player's primary play types.",
  "Optimal methods for defining 'initiation profile' or 'primary types' in the context of predicting role reallocation.",
  "The specific mechanisms by which play types are reallocated among teammates following an absence.",
  "The statistical power for detecting specific types of role reallocation with the available sample size of absence spells."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The core hypothesis of the research question, which posits a direct link between teammate similarity/usage and gaining the absent player's play types, is directly challenged by the null finding in FAM:SF-CHT-052 regarding similarity and role reallocation.",
  "The current sample size of n=11 cases (absence spells) is extremely small for detecting a specific effect, especially given the 'small_sample' limitations noted in related studies (FAM:SF-APPS-088, FAM:SF-APPS-036, FAM:SF20)."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The precise definition and measurement of 'initiation profile' and 'primary types' for both the absent player and potential gainers are not fully specified in the question, and their operationalization could significantly impact results.",
  "The specific metric for 'gainer' (e.g., absolute volume, percentage share, change from baseline) is not defined.",
  "The methodology for identifying and attributing 'absence spells' and 'primary types' is not detailed.",
  "The extent to which factors beyond player similarity/usage (e.g., coaching decisions, opponent matchups, other team personnel changes) confound the observed reallocation is a major unknown."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence, particularly FAM:SF-CHT-052, directly contradicts the core premise of the research question regarding player similarity predicting role reallocation. This strong null finding from a powered study significantly reduces the probability of a positive outcome for the current question as posed, leading to a decision not to proceed without substantial re-framing."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.1,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "The stated sample size of 11 cases (absence spells) is critically small, making it nearly impossible to detect a statistically significant effect with useful power, especially for a complex effect like role reallocation across multiple players and play types. Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPS-088, FAM:SF-APPS-036, FAM:SF20) consistently flags small sample size as a limitation for related studies."
   },
   {
    "code": "CONFOUNDING",
    "text": "Many factors beyond teammate similarity/usage influence offensive role reallocation (e.g., coach strategy, other player absences/injuries, opponent adjustments, specific game situations). Without robust controls or a quasi-experimental design, isolating the effect of 'closest profile' will be highly confounded."
   },
   {
    "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
    "text": "The definitions of 'initiation profile,' 'primary types,' 'closest,' and 'largest gainer' are open to interpretation and require careful, reliable operationalization. The reliability of these measures could significantly impact the study's validity."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
   "FAM:SF-APPS-088",
   "FAM:SF20"
  ],
  "summary": "Previous work established that player absences do cause shifts in offensive structure (FAM:SF-APPS-088, FAM:SF20) and that player-level initiation can be analyzed (FAM:SF-APPS-036). However, a direct test of whether 'similar-profile teammates' predict reallocation patterns (absorption vs. cascade) yielded an inconclusive/null result, suggesting similarity alone is not a strong predictor (FAM:SF-CHT-052). This directly undermines the central premise of the current question."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "With only 11 identified absence spells, the study will be severely underpowered to detect any meaningful effect, especially given the variability inherent in player roles and team offenses. Related studies (FAM:SF-APPS-088, FAM:SF-APPS-036, FAM:SF20) have faced this limitation even with larger samples."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Teammate role changes during an absence can be influenced by numerous factors beyond pre-absence similarity, such as coaching decisions, the specific skills of available players, opponent matchups, and the presence of other injured players. Isolating the effect of the 'closest profile' will be challenging without strong controls or a robust causal inference framework."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "Defining 'initiation profile,' 'primary types,' and 'closest' requires precise operationalization. Different metrics for similarity or 'primary types' could lead to different conclusions. The reliability and validity of these definitions are critical risks."
  },
  {
   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "The 'primary types' of an absent player might be rare or unique, leading to sparse data for measuring gains in those specific types by other players, especially for a small sample of absence spells."
  },
  {
   "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
   "text": "Players gaining roles might be selected for increased usage due to recent good performance (regression to the mean) rather than pre-absence profile similarity, especially if 'absence spells' are not randomly distributed."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.05,
  "rejected": 0.35,
  "untestable": 0.5,
  "artifact": 0.05,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of an absent player's primary play types on the change in play type initiation volume (or share) for the teammate identified as 'closest' by various pre-defined similarity metrics (initiation profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) during the absence period, compared to other teammates.",
  "unit": "Absence spell-teammate pair. An absence spell is defined as a contiguous block of games where a focal player is inactive for a minimum of X games (e.g., 3-5 games).",
  "sample": "All identified absence spells (n=11) from NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26, excluding spells where multiple key players are absent simultaneously. For each spell, identify all teammates who played during the absence.",
  "power": "Not achievable with the current sample size (n=11). A robust study would likely require hundreds of absence spells to achieve adequate power for detecting a nuanced effect like role reallocation, especially given the high dimensionality of player profiles and play types.",
  "controls": "Match each absence spell with a counterfactual period (e.g., prior to absence, or during the same season with similar team composition). Control for team-level offensive tendencies, opponent strength, other concurrent player absences/returns, and coaching changes. Use pre-absence baseline metrics for all players.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test could involve examining if 'closest' teammates also gain roles in play types *not* primary to the absent player, which would suggest a more general usage increase rather than specific reallocation. Alternatively, test if random teammate assignments show similar 'gains'.",
  "multiplicity": "Multiple definitions for 'closest' teammate (initiation profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) and 'primary types' will lead to multiple comparisons. A clear hierarchy of hypotheses or adjustment for multiple testing (e.g., Holm-Bonferroni) would be required.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis would be supported if the 'closest' teammate (as defined by the pre-specified primary metric) shows a statistically significant (p < 0.05, two-tailed) and substantively meaningful increase in the absent player's primary play types, after controlling for confounders, and this effect is significantly larger than for other teammates. Given the sample size, this is highly unlikely.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular season and playoffs, using derived play-type possession records from the NBA stats API."
 }
}

